Origin: PdfCraft AROS port, 2026-10-18, against aros_src5376f09 after patch0001.
Problem: detached completion clears ThreadInfo while StarterFunc still runs with
  scheduling enabled. __pthread_Exit_Func can observe the empty slot and return;
  unloading the executable then leaves the detached task in freed code. A live
  detached pthread at GUI shutdown led to an isolated, native RED regression.
Change: after obtaining thread_sem, forbid scheduling before publishing the empty
  slot; retain Forbid through the final return, matching the joinable path. No
  wait is added inside the protected epilogue. The semaphore acquisition remains
  outside Forbid, so a contended acquisition does not publish completion early.
Scope: private x86_64 older-v11 pthread_create.o. Shared SDK and Rust unchanged.
Verification: tests/pthread-exit widens the actual post-cleanup tail with a pure
  busy loop. Before this patch, exit returned with tail_done=0 (native rc20).
  The probe keeps its executable loaded afterwards to avoid deliberately crashing.
Upstream-status: not submitted. Mainline c31d3294 has its own changed completion/
  join/exit protocol and a final Forbid; assess separately, do not stack blindly.
User-report: post-exit desktop crash/freeze on aros.games v11 x86_64; direct GUI
  reproduction on older AROS One was negative. This race fix is not proof that
  every reported desktop failure is resolved.
---
--- a/compiler/pthread/pthread_create.c
+++ b/compiler/pthread/pthread_create.c
@@ -146,6 +146,10 @@
         Permit();
         // no one is waiting for us, do the clean up
         ObtainSemaphore(&thread_sem);
+        // Clearing the slot lets the exit handler unload this executable.
+        // Keep scheduling disabled until this task has left its code, as
+        // the joinable path above already does. ReleaseSemaphore cannot wait.
+        Forbid();
         memset(inf, 0, sizeof(ThreadInfo));
         ReleaseSemaphore(&thread_sem);
     }
